{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 359,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2739,
    "dDurationMs": 4401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can all be a little self-absorbed sometimes,\nacting like we’re the center of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7140,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or something."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8140,
    "dDurationMs": 5709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well first let me tell you where the center of the universe actually is before you decide that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13849,
    "dDurationMs": 3371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where you are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17220,
    "dDurationMs": 8460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For most of history all of humanity has been\npretty self-absorbed, astronomically speaking."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We imagined the earth the center of the cosmos until Nicolaus Copernicus shoved us from our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30640,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pedestal onto a random rocky planet orbiting\nan ordinary star in the outskirts of an unremarkable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36330,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37330,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ever since then, astronomers have embraced\nthe Copernican principle, which states that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42280,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are NOT in a special place in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44780,
    "dDurationMs": 5869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least, once you factor out the selection\nbiases of needing to be somewhere moderately"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50649,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hospitable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51649,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the Copernican principle inspired another\nimportant idea - not only are we not the center"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56899,
    "dDurationMs": 3201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe, but the universe doesn’t have a center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60100,
    "dDurationMs": 4639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once you zoom out far enough, the universe\nlooks basically the same everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64739,
    "dDurationMs": 2051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is called the Cosmological principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66790,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But neither the Copernican principle nor the\nCosmological principle are actual laws of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71310,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics - they’re philosophical positions\n- guiding principles that so far have not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77110,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "led us astray."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78210,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think both must be right - but I don’t\nknow for sure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81430,
    "dDurationMs": 3189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it sure would be nice to feel special\nagain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84619,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to ask a simple-seeming\nquestion that will lead to so pretty wacky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88590,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "places."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89590,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The question is this: If the universe has\na center, where is it?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94150,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might imagine that the center of the universe is the place where the Big Bang happened."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98330,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The origin of the explosion that created everything. This would be wrong, but it’s easy to understand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104120,
    "dDurationMs": 1929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the misconception."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106049,
    "dDurationMs": 4421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to Edwin Hubble, we know that distant\ngalaxies are racing away from us - and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110470,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "further we look, the faster they’re moving."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113040,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It looks like the Milky Way is at the center\nof an colossal explosion, or as Calvin more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117960,
    "dDurationMs": 3070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "poetically named it - a horrendous space kablooie."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121030,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Big Bang isn’t an explosion emanating\nfrom one point in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125610,
    "dDurationMs": 4879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The recession of the galaxies is just as well\nexplained if all of space is expanding evenly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130489,
    "dDurationMs": 1511,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132000,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Galaxies appear to be receding due the space\nbetween them stretching."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136730,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most importantly, this looks the same no matter\nwhat galaxy you’re in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140750,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this picture the Big Bang isn’t something\nthat happened at a single point in space,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144530,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "instead it happened everywhere at the same\ntime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147720,
    "dDurationMs": 2650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All space was created at that instant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150370,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Google “where is the center of the universe”,\nor “where did the big bang happen” and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155110,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll get this basic story for the first\n50 pages."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158660,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s because this story is probably\nright."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161040,
    "dDurationMs": 5279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today I want to dig deeper and see what\nassumptions are behind this interpretation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166319,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also ask what it would mean if those assumptions are wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169819,
    "dDurationMs": 5431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But before we mess with the standard narrative,\nlet’s make sure we understand what it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175250,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As with much, it starts with Einstein."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177530,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His general theory of relativity explains\ngravity as the warping of space and time due"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181860,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the presence of mass and energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183290,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Explains gravity as the warping of space and\ntime due to the presence of mass and energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184290,
    "dDurationMs": 3589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "General relativity can be used to calculate\nthe spacetime curvature produced by the Earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187879,
    "dDurationMs": 3271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the Sun to determine their gravitational\neffects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191150,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can also give us the gravitational field\nof the entire universe, which tells us the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197080,
    "dDurationMs": 2710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shape of all of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199790,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It wasn’t actually Einstein who worked this\nout."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202060,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first to solve this was Alexander Friedman\n- he ignored all those little gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206560,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bumps - doing the mathematical equivalent\nof grinding up everything into the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211230,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into a fine paste and spreading it evenly\nthrough space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215450,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gave him equations of motion that described\nhow the universe must evolve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219500,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a shape, defined by something called the\nFriedman-Lemaitre-Robertson-Walker metric,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225190,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also named for those three other guys who\ncame up with it right after Friedman."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229870,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This simplification really paid off."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232310,
    "dDurationMs": 4959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing it predicted that the universe\ncould not be static - it had to be contracting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237269,
    "dDurationMs": 5931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or expanding, and that as more than a decade\nbefore Hubble discovered it was the latter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243200,
    "dDurationMs": 7670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The FLRW metric also predicts that there are\nonly three possible global shapes to 4D spacetime,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250870,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "determined entirely by one number- the curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254610,
    "dDurationMs": 4909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Depending on whether the average curvature\nis positive, negative, or zero, we get one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259519,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of three shapes- which we call ‘closed’,\n‘open’, and flat.’"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264389,
    "dDurationMs": 6011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The exact shape is determined by the relative\namounts of matter and dark energy in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270400,
    "dDurationMs": 6210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The presence of matter increases the curvature\nand the presence of dark energy decreases"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276610,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277610,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t visualize the center of a geometry\nif we can’t fit it in our heads."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283340,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to do that we need to lose a dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287080,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Positively curved 3D space is the easiest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291180,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First imagine the surface of a sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293360,
    "dDurationMs": 1259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That surface is 2-dimensional."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294619,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for a 2-D being living on that surface,\nthose two dimensions are all that exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301479,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The surface is finite, but there’s no edge\nand there’s no center - or at least, no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306849,
    "dDurationMs": 3141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "center that’s part of the 2-D universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309990,
    "dDurationMs": 6659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A closed 3-D universe is like the 3-D surface\nof a 4-dimensional hypersphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316649,
    "dDurationMs": 4121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And just like it’s 2-D analog, it’s finite\nand center-less."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320770,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If I were to ask a denizen of the surface\nof the sphere to point to the center of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326319,
    "dDurationMs": 2550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe, they couldn’t do it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328869,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can’t point “down” to the center\nof the sphere, because to them there is no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333189,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334189,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But from our perspective that direction exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337379,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So could it be that there’s a higher dimensional\nspace in which our 4-D hypersphere lives?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343389,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Could there be an equivalent of “down”\nin that space that we are just too dimensionally-challenged"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349190,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to point to?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350800,
    "dDurationMs": 1699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not necessarily."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352499,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space can have curvature without there being\nanything for it to curve into."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356669,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s the most straightforward interpretation of the FLRW metric for a closed universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362729,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those three spatial dimensions just loop back on themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366729,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But actually, we can sort of give a location for this expanding hypersphere - because at one point we were"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374289,
    "dDurationMs": 2011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at that center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376300,
    "dDurationMs": 3119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So far we’ve ignored the dimension of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379419,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our universe is expanding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381199,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the closed universe, that means\nit started out as a very, very tiny hypersphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386979,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface and got bigger."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389039,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, very crudely, we can think of the radial\ndirection as the dimension of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394689,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would be more accurate to say that the\nradial dimension of this expanding hypersphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400009,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is represented in the math by the scale factor\n- and the scale factor increases as time increases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407189,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s fun to think of the center of the\nexpanding hypersphere as being a location"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411139,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412759,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means we really CAN point to the location\nof the Big Bang - by pointing to the past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419219,
    "dDurationMs": 5311,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And conveniently, you can point to the past\n- by pointing in any direction whatsoever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424530,
    "dDurationMs": 2229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m serious, hear me out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426759,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can point at, say, the moon by ensuring\nthat a line drawn from your outstretched finger"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431749,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intersects would intersect the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433069,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course your wouldn’t be pointing at the\nmoon of the present - it would be the moon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438189,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the past, because you’re aligning your\nfinger with the light that only now reached"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442599,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you from the moon, and that light has been traveling one second ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447789,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now imagine that 2-D dweller points at a random direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453069,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Draw a line in that direction and at the same\ntime reverse the flow of time to see what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458449,
    "dDurationMs": 1541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it intersects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459990,
    "dDurationMs": 5189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The line loops around the closed universe\nas the sphere shrinks, until eventually all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465179,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points in the universe, including the pointed\nline, coincide with the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469569,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the same with our universe - point\nin any direction and you’re pointing at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474289,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475839,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if the universe really is closed, you’re\nalso pointing at the point where all space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481229,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "occupied the geometrical center of the hypersphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484569,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A lot of this stuff is also true for a flat\nor open universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489149,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The 2-D analog of the flat universe is an\ninfinite flat plane, while the open universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493869,
    "dDurationMs": 5151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corresponds to a sort of saddle shape - what\nwe call a hyperbolic plane - which also stretches"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499020,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500830,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In both of these cases there is no geometrical\ncenter, even in a fictional higher dimension."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507270,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However you can still point at the Big Bang\nby pointing in a random direction, because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512180,
    "dDurationMs": 4719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the line traced from you finger also ends\nup at the beginning of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516899,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These lines we’ve been tracing have a name\n- they’re called null geodesics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521909,
    "dDurationMs": 5021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re the grid that defines the fabric\nof space time in general relativity, and correspond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526930,
    "dDurationMs": 1899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the paths followed by light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528829,
    "dDurationMs": 7161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No matter the geometry of our FLRW universe,\nall geodesics converge to a single point in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535990,
    "dDurationMs": 2289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past, and end there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538279,
    "dDurationMs": 6471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the language of GR, we call this ending\nof spacetime paths “geodesic incompleteness”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544750,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geodesic incompleteness is just a fancy way\nto say “singularity”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549529,
    "dDurationMs": 5471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is literally no direction that you could\npoint that would not intersect the Big Bang"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if traced backwards, and that’s true anywhere\nin the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559800,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We say that the Big Bang is a past, space-like\nsingularity - which means it occupies all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565120,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space at t=0 and is in the past of all paths\nthrough spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571310,
    "dDurationMs": 5569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so maybe the location of the Big Bang\nisn’t at one point in this universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576879,
    "dDurationMs": 3541,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But can we still say that the Big Bang happened\nat one point?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580420,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, if all points converged onto the same\npoint at the beginning - if all geodesics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586610,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emerged from that point - does that mean the\nuniverse started out point-like?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592330,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of the closed universe that’s\neasier to imagine - rewind the growing sphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597680,
    "dDurationMs": 3579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it approaches a single point at t=0."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601259,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about an infinite universe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603779,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The math of the FLWR metric and the Friedman\nequations tell us that as time approaches"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609949,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "zero, the distance between any two points\napproaches zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613529,
    "dDurationMs": 3441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But at the same time there are infinite points."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616970,
    "dDurationMs": 6610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So did the universe start out pointlike at\nt=0 and then suddenly become infinite in size?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623580,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well the size of the universe at t=0 is zero\ntimes infinity … which is neither zero nor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630290,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinity - it’s the point where the math\nbreaks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634130,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s the nature of singularities - they\nare discontinuities in the math we use to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638389,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describe the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640209,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They probably also represent places where\nour understanding of physics breaks apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645329,
    "dDurationMs": 3481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stand by for our theory of quantum gravity\nto resolve that one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648810,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, there are close parallels with\nthe singularity of the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653760,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We might ask whether the Big Bang is a reverse\nblack hole - also called a white hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659180,
    "dDurationMs": 1849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We might, but we won’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661029,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a topic worth it’s own video - although\nas a spoiler, the answer isn’t as obviously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666459,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the negative as you might think."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668779,
    "dDurationMs": 5401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we have the state of the current wisdom\non the shape of the universe, and the non-existence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674180,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675490,
    "dDurationMs": 3279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I promised to tell you how this might\nnot be true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678769,
    "dDurationMs": 4851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember that Alexander Friedman came up with his solution for the shape of the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683620,
    "dDurationMs": 4569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by assuming that matter and energy are evenly\nspread out everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688189,
    "dDurationMs": 5731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He assumed a homogeneous universe and assumed the cosmological principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693920,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if he was wrong?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695410,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out there are ways to make sense\nof Hubble’s observation of the receding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699620,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies that doesn’t require an infinite\nuniverse, nor a hyperspherically looping universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705700,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you can do it without breaking Einstein’s\ngeneral relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709850,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the alternative solutions was\ndiscovered by Georges Lemaitre, the L in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716680,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "FLRW metric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lemaitre asked what the universe might look\nlike if it was NOT homogeneous."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722680,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He sought solutions to the Einstein equation\nfor a universe that is lumpy on the largest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728199,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scales."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729199,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In one of his solutions, matter was distributed with constant density across a spherically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734329,
    "dDurationMs": 5331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "symmetric cloud, but beyond that cloud the\ndensity could change, or space could be empty."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739660,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He found that an observer in a sufficiently\nlarge cloud that was expanding or contracting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744310,
    "dDurationMs": 7719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would observe an expanding or contracting\nuniverse that looks exactly like a FLRW universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752029,
    "dDurationMs": 7731,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At around the same time, Richard Tolman made\nthe same discovery, and so we have the Lemaitre-Tolman"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759760,
    "dDurationMs": 4569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metric. Such a universe would have a center - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764329,
    "dDurationMs": 2841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "center of the cloud, assuming the cloud is\nfinite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767170,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should point out that this doesn’t necessarily\nbreak the Cosmological principle because it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772050,
    "dDurationMs": 5449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could still be that our bubble is small on\nthe most ridiculously gigantic scales, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777499,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that if you zoom out to many, many, many times\nlarger than the observable universe, everything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782709,
    "dDurationMs": 1471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evens out smoothly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784180,
    "dDurationMs": 7010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you can find a Lemaître-Tolman universe inside a greater FLRW universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791190,
    "dDurationMs": 6059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s even a scenario where this might\nbe the case: that’s eternal inflation, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797249,
    "dDurationMs": 6051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proposes that our universe is just one bubble\nof relatively slowly expanding space embedded"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803300,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "within an unthinkably colossal region of exponentially accelerating space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808230,
    "dDurationMs": 5359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Check out our episode on that for that insane-seeming proposition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813589,
    "dDurationMs": 4821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Long story short: the universe probably doesn’t\nhave a center, and if it does we may never"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818410,
    "dDurationMs": 6169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know - the evidence of there being a boundary\nto our bubble is very likely far beyond our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824579,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825579,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In that case then as far as we know there\ncould be a center and we could be at it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831629,
    "dDurationMs": 5411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So go ahead and get right back on that pedestal\n- we know it’s almost certainly not true,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837040,
    "dDurationMs": 6390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but no one is ever going to prove that the\nearth, humanity, even you personally, are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843430,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not at the very center of a very non-Copernican\nspacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847250,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850540,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand astrophysics, Brilliant believes\nit helps to have a solid understanding of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854930,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855930,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant has a lesson on special relativity\nthat includes interactive challenges and problems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860190,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to solve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861190,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hands-on approach can guide you through\nthinking strategies for challenging subjects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865620,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867480,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It starts out explaining relativity in terms\nof boats passing by each other on a river,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871620,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then goes into some puzzles that require changing perspective (like the ball stack bounce),"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877050,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then goes into the speed of light and\neven time travel!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881500,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To learn more about Brilliant, go to Brilliant.org/Spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886160,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re answering questions from the\nlast two episodes - the one where we explored"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890240,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Proxima system in search of habitable\nworlds, and the other one where we asked whether"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894410,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electric charge really is a fundamental property\nof nature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898470,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starting with Proxima."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899800,
    "dDurationMs": 5229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Palindromeordnijap asks whether the massive\ntides that the Proxima planets experience"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905029,
    "dDurationMs": 5091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might be ideal for life due to the rich biodiversity\nof Earth’s intertidal zones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910120,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m no expert, but I do remember that for\na while tidal pools where thought to be a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915600,
    "dDurationMs": 4239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "likely location for abiogenesis - the origin\nof life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919839,
    "dDurationMs": 4011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That seems to have fallen out of favor, with\ngeothermal vents and hot springs providing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923850,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more promising temperature gradients and general chemistries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 927100,
    "dDurationMs": 4261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even if life didn’t start in tidal pools\non Earth, who’s to say it couldn’t have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931361,
    "dDurationMs": 2008,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happened that way elsewhere?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933369,
    "dDurationMs": 6421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Relatedly, dmaxcustom asks whether the tidal\nforce wouldn’t resulted in strong tectonic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939790,
    "dDurationMs": 1719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "activity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941509,
    "dDurationMs": 4231,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tidal squeezing should indeed help keep the\nplanet’s interior hot, just like it does"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945740,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the volcanic moons of Jupiter and Saturn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948420,
    "dDurationMs": 4199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a planet that would presumable help drive\ntectonic activity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952619,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On Earth, tectonic activity is essential for\nlife due to its role in the carbon cycle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957339,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life draws carbon from the atmosphere and\nsequestered in the crust."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961699,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the crust is pulled into the hot mantle,\nthat carbon is released again in volcanic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966089,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "activity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967089,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without that recycling Earth would have frozen\ndue to the absence of greenhouse effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971610,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hot interior may also have been essential\nfor abiogenesis - if life really did first"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977410,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "start around geothermal vents or hot springs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982239,
    "dDurationMs": 5753,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in short those tidal forces on those Proxima planets may make it more likely that there's life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987992,
    "dDurationMs": 7890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Al H asks what particles and interactions\nexisted before the electroweak force split"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995882,
    "dDurationMs": 1367,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into electromagnetism and the weak force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997249,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the big one is that the elementary particles were massless back then due to the absense of the Higgs mechansism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002649,
    "dDurationMs": 6031,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, before that split, particles could transfer\ntheir isospin a lot more easily."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008680,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means there was not much of a difference\nbetween Up and Down quarks or between electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013850,
    "dDurationMs": 2039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their neutrinos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015889,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like it makes no sense to distinguish\nelectrons with up or down spin as different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1021529,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022529,
    "dDurationMs": 6091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the universe seemed to be made of\nonly six particles, three quarks and three"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1028620,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leptons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029620,
    "dDurationMs": 5841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the universe cooled down and the electroweak symmetry was broken, particles were locked in whatever isospin state "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035461,
    "dDurationMs": 2299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they happened to be in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037760,
    "dDurationMs": 3889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They gained different properties depending\non their isospin state and on their the newly gained"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1041649,
    "dDurationMs": 1030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042679,
    "dDurationMs": 5181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "TheGundeck asks whether chirality depend on\nthe observers reference frame, given that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047860,
    "dDurationMs": 4189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s defined in reference to the momentum\nvector of the particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052049,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if a particle races past you\nyou observe its chirality based on its direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1057420,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058420,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happens if you then you accelerate and overtake the particle\nso it appears to be moving in the reverse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1063610,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064610,
    "dDurationMs": 1199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does its chirality flip?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065809,
    "dDurationMs": 3921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does that mean it stops interacting with the\nweak force?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069730,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is that chirality isn’t as simple\nas a projection of spin onto the momentum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073850,
    "dDurationMs": 1079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074929,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That spin projection is actually called helicity,\nand it does change depending on your reference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1080690,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frame."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081690,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But chirality is what we call Lorentz invariant\n- it’s fundamental and doesn’t change."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085740,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s only equal to helicity for particles\nthat you can’t overtake - aka light speed,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092210,
    "dDurationMs": 1199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massless particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093409,
    "dDurationMs": 2911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sorry we didn’t have time to dig deeper\non this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096320,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1097320,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And back to Proxima for one last comment."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1100130,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anarchy Antz asks if there’s any update\non the radio signal from the direction of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1105730,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Proxima detected by the Breakthrough Listen\nproject."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108740,
    "dDurationMs": 2809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes, there’s an update."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111549,
    "dDurationMs": 7850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Radio astronomer Sofia Sheikh found around\n60 similar signals that were not clearly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1119399,
    "dDurationMs": 7241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "associated with stars and had radio frequency\nspacing similar to Earthly oscillators."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126640,
    "dDurationMs": 3169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it looks like local interferenced."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1129809,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or in other words, the Proximans realized\nwe were onto them and hacked the database"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134899,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and injected fake signals to put us off their\ntracks."
    } ]
  } ]
}
